    "created_at": "2016-02-26T18:53:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-02-26T18:53:08Z",
    "body": "Let's do line-by-line, it's clearer:\n\n> When the details binding applies to a details element, the element is expected to render as a 'block' box with its 'padding-left' property set to '40px' for left-to-right elements (LTR-specific) and with its 'padding-right' property set to '40px' for right-to-left elements. \n\nThis part appears to have been ignored by implementations, and can be dropped.  (Or if it's retained, it can be converted into real CSS using `padding-inline-start: 40px;`.)\n\n> The element's shadow tree is expected to take the element's first child summary element, if any, and place it in a first 'block' box container, and then take the element's remaining descendants, if any, and place them in a second 'block' box container.\n\nThis needs to be kept.\n\n> The first container is expected to contain at least one line box,\n\nUnclear what this means.  Inline content will generate a line box naturally, but I don't understand what this requirement is meant to do if the `<summary>` is empty.\n\n> and that line box is expected to contain a disclosure widget (typically a triangle), horizontally positioned within the left padding of the details element. \n\nThis should be replaceable with real CSS by using `list-style`, as outlined above.\n\n> That widget is expected to allow the user to request that the details be shown or hidden.\n\nKeep, obviously. It's worded in an awkwardly generic way, tho - the mechanism for open/closed determination in `<details>` is whether or not the [open] attribute is specified, and clicking the `<summary>` is specified to add/remove that attribute by default.\n\nIs this just meant to be clarifying that clicking the widget itself also opens/closes the element, not just clicking the `<summary>` contents>? If the widget is rephrased into utilizing pseudo-elements, this happens automatically, as events move up to the originating element.\n\n> The second container is expected to have its 'overflow' property set to 'hidden'. \n\nI don't understand how this is supposed to be observable. You can't target the second box to apply a height, so it'll always be content-height and overflow will never apply.  (The `<details>` element can overflow, obviously, but that's affected by the `overflow` property on the `<details>` element, not its contents.)  Should be removable without replacement.\n\n> When the details element does not have an open attribute, this second container is expected to be removed from the rendering.\n\nKeep.\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
